 ## Overview
-DCx is an isosurface extraction method designed to reconstruct zero-level sets from neural Unsigned Distance Functions (UDFs), addressing the lack of sign information inherent in unsigned fields [S1]. Unlike standard Dual Contouring, which relies on sign changes to identify active cubes and connect vertices, DCx uses an optimization-based strategy to determine active cubes and operates on 2x2x2 voxel blocks (expanded cubes) with a voxel-to-mesh lookup table to handle sparse, irregular active cube distributions and non-manifold structures [S1]. It produces single-layer, intersection-free meshes for both manifold and non-manifold inputs without requiring supervision, avoiding the double-layer entanglement and topological errors common in prior UDF extraction methods [S1].
+DCx is an isosurface extraction method designed to reconstruct zero-level sets from neural Unsigned Distance Functions (UDFs), addressing the lack of sign information inherent in unsigned fields [S1]. Unlike standard Dual Contouring which relies on sign changes to identify active cubes and connect vertices, DCx uses an optimization-based strategy for active cube detection and operates on 2x2x2 voxel blocks (expanded cubes) to robustly handle sparse, irregular active cube distributions and non-manifold structures [S1]. It produces single-layer, intersection-free meshes for both manifold and non-manifold inputs without requiring supervision, avoiding the double-layer entanglement and topological errors common in prior UDF extraction methods [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** A neural Unsigned Distance Function (UDF) $u(\mathbf{x})$ defined on a regular grid of resolution $N \times N \times N$ [S1].
-*   **Output:** A single-layer triangle mesh representing the zero-level set $\{ \mathbf{x} \mid u(\mathbf{x}) = 0 \}$ [S1].
+**Inputs:**
+- A neural Unsigned Distance Function (UDF) $u(\mathbf{x})$ defined on a 3D grid.
+- Grid resolution $N$ (e.g., 256).
+- Thresholds $\tau_0$ and $\tau_1$ for active cube detection and supplementary sampling.
+
+**Outputs:**
+- A single-layer triangle mesh representing the zero-level set of the UDF.
 
 **Data Structures:**
-*   **Grid:** Regular 3D grid with $N^3$ voxels. Each voxel stores the UDF value at its centroid [G].
-*   **Expanded Cube:** A 2x2x2 block of voxels [S1].
-*   **Dual Cube:** The 8 vertices of an expanded cube, treated as vertices in the dual space [S1].
-*   **V2M Lookup Table:** A mapping from the binary configuration of active/inactive voxels within an expanded cube to the mesh connectivity (faces/edges) on the dual cube vertices [S1].
-*   **Point Set:** A set of 3D points $\mathcal{P}$ approximating the zero-level set [S1].
+- **Grid:** A 3D array of size $N \times N \times N$ storing UDF values.
+- **Expanded Cube:** A 2x2x2 block of voxels. Adjacent expanded cubes overlap, sharing 2x2x1 voxels (4 voxels in common).
+- **V2M Lookup Table:** A mapping from the binary configuration of 8 voxels (active/inactive) within an expanded cube to the corresponding mesh connectivity (faces/edges) on the dual cube vertices.
+- **Active Cube Set:** A set of cube indices identified as active based on the optimization step.
+- **Point Set:** A set of 3D points $\mathbf{p}$ representing projected centroids.
 
